Tiles for monsters Angband does not have
========================================

ZangbandTK adds 391 monsters to Angband's 624, and none of them appear in any
of the five tilesets that ship with the game.  In graphics mode they used to
render as ASCII letters among the pictures.

    scripts/tiles/borrow-tiles.py            regenerate
    scripts/tiles/borrow-tiles.py --dry-run  report without writing
    scripts/tiles/borrow-tiles.py --check    verify what was written

It writes graf-ztk.prf into each tileset's directory and adds one %: line to
the tileset's own prf so it is loaded last.  Nothing hand-authored is touched,
and a generated line only ever covers a monster that had no tile at all.

How a tile is chosen
--------------------

The monster must share a `base:` with an Angband monster the tileset already
draws -- a snake never borrows from a dog.  Sharing the `color:` too is much
better, because colour is most of what a small tile says.  Nearest `depth:`
breaks the tie, and that part matters: without it Bloodfang the Wolf borrowed a
scruffy little dog.

Uniques are left alone
----------------------

Deliberately, and it is the most important thing here.  A borrowed tile is a
fair stand-in for a common monster and a lie about a named one -- and the lie
is bad in a specific way.  Matched on base and depth, Oberon of Amber, Dworkin
Barimen and Mandor all come out wearing the Mouth of Sauron's face.  For a game
whose whole purpose is to move away from that, three Amber princes in Tolkien's
clothing is worse than no picture at all.  An ASCII letter says "not drawn
yet".  A wrong portrait says something false.

The 122 imported uniques therefore render as text until somebody draws them.

Two ways this goes wrong quietly
--------------------------------

`lookup_monster()` falls back to a substring match, so a misspelled name binds
to whatever happens to contain it.  And the renderer takes the cell index
modulo the sheet size, so an out-of-range coordinate draws a different picture
instead of failing.  Neither produces an error message.

Both are avoided by construction -- names are read from the data files and
coordinates are copied verbatim from lines the tileset already uses -- and
--check is what proves it.

Replacing a placeholder with real art
-------------------------------------

Delete the monster's line from graf-ztk.prf and add a proper mapping to the
tileset's own prf.  Re-running the script will not put it back: it only writes
lines for monsters that have no tile from any other file.
